A||||18||84sleepr||Stalling problem!!!????||||5||
Z||000000||84sleepr||11-26-2001||10:10 PM||||Please help!  Here's my problem...<P>I have an '84 SE, It starts up fine every morning and runs beautifully until I hit operating temperature...At that point it stalls any time I hit a light or stop sign.<P>It is relatively hard to start up everytime after it stalls and blows smoke.  If I let it sit and cool and start it up again it runs fine until I hit operating temperature again. <P>Any ideas at where to start?  Anyone familiar with this problem?  Please help!||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phil||11-26-2001||11:03 PM||||Is it an Automatic? ||||reg||1
Z||000002||84sleepr||11-26-2001||11:47 PM||||Yeah.. Automatic.  Any ideas??<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||84sleepr||11-27-2001||07:29 PM||||Anyone???||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||11-27-2001||08:33 PM||||Do a search for TCC , lockup solenoid , Sounds as though you could have a problem with that unit. Quick check is to disconnect it and see if the problem goes away.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Old Lar||11-27-2001||08:58 PM||||I go with Phil: torque converter solinoid.  Thats how my car ran (or didn't run).||||reg||1
Z||000006||GTDude||11-27-2001||09:45 PM||||TCC makes alot of sense except for the smoke and it would not make it hard to start.  I'd check the coolant temp sensor.  Is the smoke black or white?  Im a GM tech....maybe I can help.||||reg||1
Z||000007||84sleepr||11-27-2001||11:52 PM||||The smoke is black, I believe it is oil burning, I can't figure out myself how this would be related to the stalling at operating temp??||||reg||1
Z||000008||84sleepr||11-27-2001||11:53 PM||||Oh yeah, Where exactly is the TCC solenoid located? <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||GTDude||11-28-2001||07:47 AM||||If the smoke is black then you have a def. flooding problem.  TCC is not likely involved.  If it's a V6 could be coolant sensor...or map sensor.....or cold start solenoid.  But it is def fuel related if the smoke is black.||||reg||1
Z||000010||84sleepr||11-29-2001||12:08 AM||||Ok, I had the TCC solenoid disconnected today, and there was no change.  The car still stalled at operating temperature when at a stop sign or light.  The check engine light didn't come on or anything and it started up right away. It didn't smoke that much, but the exhaust did smell very rich...<P>I could scan it at work tomorrow but without a code I don't really know what to look for.<P>Does anyone have any suggestions on what I should do next?||||reg||9
Z||000011||84Bill||11-29-2001||09:35 AM||||Here is a few ideas<BR>Sounds like it's running rich (black smoke) If it were oil the smoke would be white.<BR>things that can cause that are the O2 sensor and Idle Air Control valve. Also clogged airfilter and wacked timing can cause stalling problems.<P>Since the problem only happens when warmed up (closed Loop) then chances are good that it is sensor related.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000012||falconhulk||11-29-2001||10:32 AM||||*$ bill oil burning i blue smoke and coolant is white.  just FYI||||reg||1
Z||000013||84Bill||11-29-2001||10:58 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by falconhulk:<BR><B>*$ bill oil burning is blue smoke and coolant is white.  just FYI</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Had an 86 2.5 with bad valve guide seals and the smoke was white and puffy like clouds on a warm summer day.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||Mach10||11-29-2001||06:58 PM||||O2 Sensor... The thing has to reach operating temperature before the ECM starts to even look at it. When you're coasting, the O2 sensor (being bad) will start giving the ECM bad data (too lean) and the ECM will dump a liter or so of fuel it. This of course stalls your car. When you start again, the engine STILL reads Too Lean, and dumps WAY too much gas in it. But because it's idleing, the ECM, sensing the bad idle, will start playing with the IAC, too...<P>Result? SHITTY warm idle, frequent stalls, and MONSTROUS gas mileage...<P>We changed ICE's O2 for about $40 and it made a world of difference....<P><BR>Anyhoo, nice to see more Pegger Drivers. Kinda sorry that we've missed you these past few weeks. We REALLY have to organize a get-together that PEOPLE CAN ACTUALLY SHOW UP TO  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Ta!||||reg||10
Z||000015||johnt671||11-29-2001||08:06 PM||||I'd changed the ox sensor and the coolant temp sensor that sends info to the ECM.  I agree with the above post and if you have a bad coolant sensor the ECM will think the car is still cold and run to rich.This sensor is the one on the drivers side between the trans and the distributor.  ||||reg||1
Z||000016||84sleepr||11-29-2001||10:19 PM||||Thanks for the responses guys,<BR>I've already replaced the coolant temp sensor and that seemed to improve things, I'm not too worried about the smoke right now cus it doesn't seem to be doing it anymore.  I'll pick up an O2 sensor tomorrow and give it a try,  I'll also check the timing,  I'll let you know what happens.<P>Thanks<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||Ferrari_cdn||11-30-2001||02:58 AM||||02 sensor did help my car run smoother, for sure.  But didn't help my cold performance.   But working on that.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ferrari_cdn" TARGET=_blank>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ferrari_cdn.JPG">   </A>  <BR>AIM: CanadianFiero||||reg||1
Z||000018||fierospeeder||11-30-2001||06:10 AM||||a stuck egr valve can kill your car also.<P><BR>also check your plug wires,  on my 84, they use to crack and i wouldn't be getting proper ignition firing that caused a rich mixture.||||reg||1
